{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Then Moses sang, and the children of Israel, this song to HaShem.</b> Why did he not sing immediately when they went out from Egypt? Because Moses knew that the children of Israel were still wavering in faith, until he saw at the sea that they believed in HaShem and in Moses His servant — then he sang. And he began with 'אָז,' that is, א riding upon ז, a hint to His enthronement, blessed be He, upon the seven wandering stars, as it is written ״כִּי גָאֹה גָּאָה״ (Exodus 15:1) — He exalts Himself above all who are exalted. And as for what is said ״הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״ in the feminine form, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 23:11) that all the songs of this world are uttered in the feminine, because after them comes suffering, like females who have the suffering of childbirth. And further, females do not receive in this world except a tithe of possessions; so too HaShem bequeathed to Israel seven nations out of seventy. But concerning the future it is written ״שִׁירוּ לַה׳ שִׁיר חָדָשׁ״ (Isaiah 42:10), for then they will be like males who do not give birth, and then they will inherit all seventy nations, like a male who inherits everything. And perhaps for this reason it begins with 'אָז,' א riding upon ז, to hint that in this world Israel is 'גּוֹי אֶחָד בָּאָרֶץ' (II Samuel 7:23) — HaShem mounts him upon the high places of earth belonging to seven nations alone, like females." "And in order to settle the superfluity of ״הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״ — for it should have said 'Then Moses sang, and the children of Israel, to HaShem' — I say that it contains a hint to what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Mekhilta Beshallah 3): 'A maidservant saw at the sea what Ezekiel did not see.' And concerning this wonder it is said ״אֶת הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״, meaning: the song of 'זֹאת' the feminine, for the females too said ״זֶה אֵלִי וְאַנְוֵהוּ״, and this matter was a wonder in their eyes — that a female should encircle a male, after the manner of the time to come. And perhaps it is concerning this that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 91b): 'It does not say שָׁר but rather יָשִׁיר — from here is a hint to the resurrection of the dead from the Torah.' And what does this song have to do with the resurrection of the dead? Rather, just as in the world to come all will be stripped of matter and then males and females will be equal, so too at the sea even the maidservant saw the splendor of His glory, blessed be He, after the manner of the time to come, of which it is written (Jeremiah 31:22) ״נְקֵבָה תְּסוֹבֵב גָּבֶר״. Therefore it is said of this song ״יָשִׁיר״ in the future tense, for just as they all said ״זֶה אֵלִי וְאַנְוֵהוּ״, so all of them will say in the future ״הִנֵּה אֱלֹהֵינוּ זֶה״ (Isaiah 25:9), as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 23:15) that the righteous will point at Him with a finger, etc. And concerning this wonder it is said ״אֶת הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״. Therefore it says ״וַתַּעַן לָהֶם מִרְיָם״ etc., as one speaking to males, as will be explained shortly." "And in the Midrash (Tanhuma Beshallah 12) it says that the sea split in the merit of circumcision — it is possible that concerning this it is said ״אֶת הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״, a hint to circumcision, as it is written (Genesis 17:10) ״זֹאת בְּרִיתִי״ etc. And since circumcision also saves from Gehinnom in the world to come, therefore ״יָשִׁיר״ is said in the future tense, and from here is a hint to the resurrection of the dead." "<b>I will sing to HaShem, for He is highly exalted.</b> He exalts Himself above all who are exalted — but not above the humble, for HaShem dwells with the crushed and lowly, and shows His lowliness among them. And it immediately adjoins ״סוּס וְרוֹכְבוֹ רָמָה בַיָּם״, for the rider is proud over the horse; therefore He cast both of them into the sea. And in order to settle according to the grammar the expression ״גָאֹה גָּאָה״, I say that it contains a hint to what was explained (Job 12:23) ״מַשְׂגִּיא לַגּוֹיִם וַיְאַבְּדֵם״, and as it is written (Psalms 92:8) ״בִּפְרֹחַ רְשָׁעִים כְּמוֹ עֵשֶׂב וַיָּצִיצוּ כָּל פֹּעֲלֵי אָוֶן לְהִשָּׁמְדָם עֲדֵי עַד״ — for whoever is already exalted, the Holy One, blessed be He, adds pride upon his pride and gives him still greater ascendancy, in order to magnify his fall from a high rooftop into a deep pit, as will be explained shortly on the verse ״אָמַר אוֹיֵב אֶרְדּוֹף״ (Exodus 15:9) — see there. This is what it says: ״אָשִׁירָה לַה׳ כִּי גָאֹה גָּאָה״, meaning: that man who has become exalted — then the Holy One, blessed be He, is also 'exalted': He adds pride to him and mounts him upon the high places of earth, but in the end ״סוּס וְרוֹכְבוֹ רָמָה בַיָּם״, meaning: to magnify the fall of the rider, for from a high place he falls, and his fall is greater than the fall of the ridden."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:34.866477605Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}